@@ -52,3 +52,29 @@ out of range, an attempt to remove the whole document, or a move into one of its
 The input document is never modified. The result is a new value that shares no object or array with
 the input or with the patch, so the caller can change it without affecting either. Values are compared
 by content in `test`: member order in an object does not matter, element order in an array does.
+
+### JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7386)
+
+`applyMergePatch(target, patch)` applies a JSON Merge Patch to a JSON document and returns the merged
+document. It suits clients that send partial updates as a plain JSON object, which says what to change
+without the list of operations that JSON Patch needs. A member of the patch that is an object is merged
+into the target member of the same name, and any other member replaces it. A member whose value is
+`null` removes the member from the target, so `null` cannot itself be stored through a merge patch.
+
+```ts
+import { applyMergePatch } from "json-toolkit";
+
+const document = { title: "Draft", tags: ["a"], owner: { name: "Ada", email: "ada@example.com" } };
+applyMergePatch(document, { title: "Final", tags: ["b"], owner: { email: null } });
+// { title: "Final", tags: ["b"], owner: { name: "Ada" } }
+```
+
+An array in the patch replaces the target's array as a whole, and any `null` inside it is kept, since
+the merge only looks at object members. When the target is not an object, and the patch is an object, the
+target is treated as an empty object. A patch that is not an object, including an array, replaces the
+target outright.
+
+Unlike JSON Patch, a merge patch has no invalid form: any JSON value is a valid patch. It throws an
+`Error` only when the target or the patch is not JSON data (a `Date`, a non-finite number, `undefined`
+or an object that contains itself). The inputs are never modified, and the result shares no object or
+array with them, as with `applyPatch`.
